Is your agent still just the thing that writes code?

@thorstenball says the latest models let you aim much higher:

“Previously the agents went fully vertical in the stack… But now I think it's time to go horizontal, meaning the whole lifecycle.” https://t.co/KYGDZG12b9

Don't wait for your agent. Let it wait for you.

@thorstenball on why he never watches agents work: let them take as long as they need to comprehensively test changes in orbs, and focus on something else.

They don’t take up resources, or cost anything while they wait. We get back to them once we’re ready.

“I thought you guys were all about non-local dev, so why do you have the runners?”

@thorstenball on runners, which run Amp agents on machines you own: a Raspberry Pi at home, a lab of test hardware, GPU boxes, a macOS build machine.

“I think runners on different machines are non-local too, you know?”

“Code review in so many companies, it's a farce now. Nobody can review the volume of code, and they're not even the best at reviewing it.”

@sqs and @thorstenball on rethinking the old rules for working with agents:

“I wish I could do this, but we don't have a monorepo… then get a monorepo.”

How do you make your codebase more agent-friendly?

@thorstenball had an agent built a dev-only login route, documented it in AGENTS.md, then spawned three blank-slate agents (and a control group) to check they’d actually use it.

“If you tell them, ‘This is the thing I want to test. Help me spawn and test other agents,’ they know how to write instructions.”

You don't need a PhD to run evals.

@sqs had agents build fake shopfronts and permit sites, complete with annoying cookie banners, to improve browser use in orbs.

“It's like with usability testing: you learn most of the insights from the first three people you talk to” https://t.co/u7S5BLrXYf

